.TH PACQ 1 "2026-07-08" "pacfz 1.0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pacq \- non\-interactive query into the pacman package database
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pacq
[OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pacq
is the non\-interactive, scriptable counterpart to
.BR pacfz (1).
It shares the exact same query engine and cache, and prints matching
package names, one per line, with no picker and no extra formatting.
.PP
Everything
.B pacq
prints goes to standard output as plain text, suitable for piping into
other commands (
.BR grep (1),
.BR xargs (1),
and so on). Because of that,
.B pacq
does not support
.B \-\-verbose
(see below), and its output never includes descriptive prose or color
codes the way
.BR pacfz (1)
does.
.PP
The package cache builds itself automatically on first use, in the
same location
.BR pacfz (1)
uses.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Filter options
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-foreign
Only foreign (AUR / non\-repo) packages.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-orphans
Only orphan packages: installed as a dependency, and now required by
nothing. Does not consider optional\-dependency relationships; see
.B "A NOTE ON ORPHAN"
below.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-explicit
Only explicitly installed packages.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-deps
Only packages installed as a dependency.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-notadep
Only packages not required by anything.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-notoptdep
Only packages that are not an optional dependency for anything.
.TP
.B \-\-has\-note
Only packages that have a user note attached.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-note
Only packages without a user note.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIFIELD VALUE\fR, \fB\-\-field\fR \fIFIELD VALUE\fR
Only packages where
.I FIELD
matches
.IR VALUE .
Repeatable; multiple
.B \-f
conditions are combined with AND. Matching is substring by default;
see
.B \-x
below.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR \fIVALUE\fR, \fB\-\-any\fR \fIVALUE\fR
Only packages where any field matches
.IR VALUE .
Repeatable, and combined with AND against everything else active.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-exact\-match
Use exact equality instead of substring matching for
.B \-f
and
.BR \-a .
.TP
\fB\-v\fR \fR[\fIFILTER\fR]\fR, \fB\-\-invert\fR \fR[\fIFILTER\fR]
Invert results. Bare
.B \-v
inverts the entire query, applied last, as a single unit. Given a
.IR FILTER ,
.B \-v
instead inverts only that one canned filter, leaving every other
active filter in place as a precondition; repeatable. See
.B "INVERTING RESULTS"
below.
.SS Sort options
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fR[\fIFIELD\fR]\fR, \fB\-\-sort\-by\fR \fR[\fIFIELD\fR]
Sort by
.IR FIELD .
Default field is
.IR Name ;
bare
.B \-s
with no field sorts by
.IR Installed_Size .
.B \-\-size
is a synonym for this option.
.TP
.B \-\-asc
Sort ascending. Applies to whichever field is being sorted.
.TP
.B \-\-desc
Sort descending.
.SS Size
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-totalsize
Print the total installed size of exactly the packages selected by
the other active filters, not the whole system.
.SS Other
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-refresh
Rebuild the package cache from
.BR pacman (8)
and exit, silently (no summary, no output at all). Use
.B "pacfz \-r"
if you want a summary printed.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the installed pacfz version and exit.
.SH OPTIONS NOT AVAILABLE IN PACQ
A few flags exist in
.BR pacfz (1)
but are intentionally absent here, since they either control the
interactive picker directly or produce output that isn't meant to be
piped:
.B \-D
(note management),
.BR \-\-notes\-list ,
.BR \-\-exact ,
.BR \-\-hide\-notes ,
and
.BR \-\-verbose .
Passing
.B \-\-verbose
specifically is a hard error (exit status 1) rather than being
silently ignored, since it would otherwise print debug output onto
what's meant to be clean, scriptable stdout.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including the case where the query matched no packages
(prints nothing at all).
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred: an invalid flag combination, a database error, or
.B \-\-verbose
was passed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pacq \-m
List foreign packages.
.TP
.B pacq \-o
List orphans.
.TP
.B pacq \-m \-t
Print the total installed size of just the foreign packages.
.TP
.B pacq \-f Name vim
List packages whose name contains "vim".
.TP
.B pacq \-e \-s \-\-desc
List explicitly installed packages, largest first.
.TP
.B "pacq \-e | wc \-l"
Count how many packages are explicitly installed.
.SH INVERTING RESULTS
Bare
.B \-v
negates everything else you specified, as one unit, applied last:
.PP
.RS
.EX
pacq \-m \-v            (NOT foreign)
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB\-v\fR \fIFILTER\fR instead negates only that one named filter,
leaving every other active filter in place as a precondition, and is
repeatable:
.PP
.RS
.EX
pacq \-e \-v orphan                   (explicit, and NOT an orphan)
pacq \-e \-v notadep \-v notoptdep     (explicit, and NOT
                                      (notadep AND notoptdep))
.EE
.RE
.PP
.I FILTER
is one of
.IR foreign ", " orphan ", " explicit ", " deps ", " notadep ", "
.IR notoptdep ,
or its short letter:
.IR m / o / e / d / n / k .
.SH A NOTE ON ORPHAN
Here,
.I orphan
means: installed as a dependency, and now required by nothing
(equivalent to
.BR "pacman \-Qdt" ).
It does
.B not
consider optional\-dependency relationships, so a package that is
.I only
an optional dependency of something else still counts as an orphan.
Combine with
.B \-k
to additionally exclude those:
.BR "pacq \-o \-k" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PACFZ_DB_PATH
Overrides the package cache DB path outright. Default:
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/pacman_packages.db .
.TP
.B PACFZ_NOTES_DB_PATH
Overrides the notes DB path outright. Default:
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/pacfz/user_data.db .
Only relevant to
.B pacq
through
.B \-\-has\-note
/
.BR \-\-no\-note ;
.B pacq
itself never reads or writes note text.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/pacman_packages.db
The package metadata cache, shared with
.BR pacfz (1),
rebuilt from
.BR pacman (8)
output.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pacfz (1),
.BR pactui (1),
.BR pacman (8)
.SH AUTHOR
marco-l
